cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "fsQCA Calibrator for Likert data"
message: >-
  Scientific credit for the computations belongs to the packages and the works
  cited below, not to this tool: it reimplements no statistical formula. Cite
  this repository only to identify the instrument an analysis was run with,
  and name the git tag or commit you used: this repository has no release
  numbering beyond its tags, so a citation without one identifies nothing.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Núñez"
    given-names: "Javier"
repository-code: "https://github.com/jnverbel/fsqca-calibrador"
url: "https://github.com/jnverbel/fsqca-calibrador"
abstract: >-
  A Shiny application that takes a file of five-point Likert responses to a
  methodological appendix for fsQCA, in eight steps: ingest, measurement
  validation, aggregation, direct fuzzy calibration, diagnostic traffic
  light, necessity and sufficiency analysis, robustness sweep and export.
  Conditions may be declared crisp, and directional expectations may be
  declared condition by condition so that the intermediate solution is
  reachable and its provenance is recorded. Every computation is delegated to
  peer-reviewed packages. Its contribution is to force every calibration
  decision to be justified in writing at the moment it is taken, and to print
  that text in the final report. Its six analytical modules have been
  contrasted, module by module, against eight published fsQCA studies through
  the package's public functions; the coverage is modular and there is no
  integral external validation of the complete Likert/multi-item workflow.
  Figures, deviations and limits are in docs/validacion-integral.md. The
  interface and the generated report are in Spanish.
keywords:
  - fsQCA
  - QCA
  - calibration
  - fuzzy sets
  - Likert data
  - survey research
  - replication
  - external validation
  - R
  - Shiny
license: MIT
